<article>
<h1>An odd-period product for focal inversions of outer billiard polygons</h1>
<p class="entry-meta">Alec Kriebel · Independent researcher · <time datetime="2026-10-15">15 October 2026</time> · Zenodo version 1.0 · Unrefereed preprint</p>
<p><a rel="author" href="https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9320-500X">ORCID: 0009-0001-9320-500X</a> · <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23394004">Zenodo record and DOI</a></p>
<p>A periodic billiard orbit traces a polygon inside an ellipse. Intersecting tangents at consecutive vertices produces an outer polygon. This paper inverts those outer vertices about each of the ellipse's two foci and proves that, for odd least periods, the two resulting signed areas have a constant positive product as the orbit moves around its family.</p>

<section id="original-abstract" aria-labelledby="abstract-heading">
<h2 id="abstract-heading">Original manuscript abstract</h2>
<p>For a periodic billiard family between an ellipse and a strictly nested nondegenerate confocal elliptical caustic, form the outer polygon by intersecting consecutive tangents at the billiard vertices. Invert its vertices in the unit circles centered at the two original billiard foci, and join successive images by straight segments. We prove that the product of the resulting signed areas is constant for every odd least period, including primitive stars. This proves the invariant conjectured as k904,a in the pinned 2020 list of Reznik, Garcia and Koiller. An explicit inverse-edge factorization reduces the area to a cyclic elliptic sum. Its double principal parts cancel, leaving two simple poles and complementary half-period zeros. The triangular critical case and repeated traversals are included. The analytic methods are classical; no first-proof or absolute-priority claim is made. This AI-assisted research note is unrefereed.</p>
</section>

<section id="paper" aria-labelledby="paper-heading">
<h2 id="paper-heading">Read the paper</h2>
<p><a href="k904a-outer-focal-inverse-areas.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the original PDF</a> · <a href="k904a-outer-focal-inverse-areas.pdf" download>Download the PDF</a> · <a href="citation.bib" download>BibTeX</a></p>
<iframe class="paper-pdf" src="k904a-outer-focal-inverse-areas.pdf#view=FitH" title="Original six-page manuscript: An odd-period product for focal inversions of outer billiard polygons"></iframe>
<p>The unchanged manuscript is dated 15 October 2026. The Zenodo record was created that day in UTC and identifies version 1.0, also recorded in the authored release metadata. The manuscript does not print a version number.</p>
</section>

<section id="result" aria-labelledby="result-heading">
<h2 id="result-heading">Which vertices, which foci and which area?</h2>
<p>Fix a noncircular ellipse with semiaxes a &gt; b &gt; 0 and original foci f<sub>±</sub> = (±c, 0), where c<sup>2</sup> = a<sup>2</sup> − b<sup>2</sup>. A billiard family shares a strictly nested, nondegenerate confocal elliptical caustic. If P<sub>j</sub> are successive billiard vertices, let Q<sub>j</sub> be the intersection of the tangents to the original ellipse at P<sub>j</sub> and P<sub>j+1</sub>.</p>
<p>For each original focus f, invert the outer vertices in the unit circle centered at f:</p>
<p class="equation">J<sub>f</sub>(Q) = f + (Q − f) / ‖Q − f‖<sup>2</sup>.</p>
<p>Join successive image vertices by <strong>straight segments</strong>. For the two resulting ordered polygons, define signed shoelace areas</p>
<p class="equation">B<sub>±</sub> = ½ ∑<sub>j=0</sub><sup>N−1</sup> det(J<sub>f±</sub>(Q<sub>j</sub>), J<sub>f±</sub>(Q<sub>j+1</sub>)).</p>
<p><strong>Theorem.</strong> Whenever the family has least odd period N ≥ 3, the product B<sub>+</sub>B<sub>−</sub> is a positive constant independent of the starting vertex. Every admitted coprime star turning number is included, and both inversions are defined at every real outer vertex.</p>
<p>Signed area follows the ordered trajectory, including self-crossing stars; it is not the area of the union of the faces. Inverting whole edges into arcs is a different construction. So are inversions of the original billiard vertices and inversions about the outer locus's own foci.</p>
</section>

<section id="explanation" aria-labelledby="explanation-heading">
<h2 id="explanation-heading">The mechanism behind the product</h2>
<p>Classical Jacobi coordinates give the outer tangent intersections explicitly. Every real outer vertex lies outside the original ellipse, while both original foci lie inside it, so no vertex meets an inversion center. The outer locus may have its major axis reversed; the argument does not assume a particular order for its two semiaxes.</p>
<p>The proof factors the actual inverse-edge area, then sums it cyclically around the orbit. In the complex continuation, double principal parts cancel. Only two simple poles remain on a smaller torus, with two complementary half-period zeros. The critical triangular case is included: a coincident pair of possible poles is reduced by a zero in the numerator.</p>
<p>Central reflection relates the two focus inversions. For odd least period, their phase difference is a half-period of the cyclic area function. That shift interchanges its zero and pole divisors, making the product constant. Positivity follows from the real edge formula. This is an analytic all-period argument; finite checks and numerical diagnostics support the constructions rather than replace the proof.</p>
</section>

<section id="scope" aria-labelledby="scope-heading">
<h2 id="scope-heading">Reversal, repetitions, circles and scale</h2>
<p>Reversing the trajectory negates both signed areas and leaves the product unchanged. Traversing an odd least-period cycle r times multiplies each area by r and the product by r<sup>2</sup>. The hypothesis concerns the least period, not the parity of a repeated vertex list. No unrestricted even-least-period theorem or assertion for collapsed or hyperbolic caustics is made.</p>
<p>A circle is handled separately by rigid rotation. With billiard radius a and forward step angle 2πτ/N in (0, π), the outer radius is R<sub>o</sub> = a/cos(πτ/N). Each central-inverse polygon has signed area N sin(2πτ/N)/(2R<sub>o</sub><sup>2</sup>), so the product is</p>
<p class="equation">[N sin(2πτ/N) / (2R<sub>o</sub><sup>2</sup>)]<sup>2</sup>.</p>
<p>The unit inversion radius matters. Scaling the entire original configuration by s &gt; 0 while retaining unit inversion scales the product by s<sup>−4</sup>. Replacing both unit inversion radii by ρ scales the product by ρ<sup>8</sup>.</p>
</section>

<section id="context" aria-labelledby="context-heading">
<h2 id="context-heading">The conjectured invariant and prior methods</h2>
<p>Reznik, Garcia and Koiller listed this assertion as k904,a in arXiv:2004.12497v11, §3.9 and Table 10, dated 29 October 2020. The same assertion was k808,a in v10, Table 9, dated 25 October 2020. The paper proves that pinned assertion in the domain above; it makes no first-proof, absolute-priority or current-worldwide-open claim.</p>
<p>Stachel's canonical coordinates, Jacobi identities, cyclic elliptic methods and complex billiard techniques are established inputs. Chavez-Caliz already used complementary area zeros and poles for untransformed Poncelet and tangent polygons. The contribution here is the specific original-focus outer-edge expression, cancellation and critical-case analysis.</p>
<p>Earlier original-vertex products, including the triangular work of Garcia, Reznik and Helman and Ferudun's odd-period counterpart, concern different vertices. The original billiard foci are generally not the foci of the outer locus. The unproved inverse-area-ratio bridge is not assumed, and related inverse-perimeter results do not by themselves prove this area product.</p>
</section>

